## Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why this breaks

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `ThreadAbortException`, `ResetAbort()` — all gone. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation**.

### Pattern classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `GetNextItem()` blocking call | Blocking call interruption | Pass token to unblock |

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Replaces ThreadAbortException catch — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop (replaces ResetAbort + loop re-check)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't stop — log a warning.
            // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort in modern .NET.
            // If GetNextItem/ProcessItem can't observe the token,
            // move them to a child process and use Process.Kill.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key changes explained

1. **`volatile bool _running` → `CancellationToken`** — Token is thread-safe by design and can interrupt blocking calls.

2. **`Thread.Abort()` → `_cts.Cancel()`** — Signals cancellation cooperatively; the loop must check it.

3. **`ThreadAbortException` → `OperationCanceledException`** — The `when` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not an unrelated one.

4. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → `break`** — ResetAbort kept the thread alive to re-enter the loop. Now you explicitly control flow with `break`.

5. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `_workerThread.Join(2s)`** — Actually waits for the thread to finish instead of blindly sleeping.

6. **`GetNextItem()` must accept `CancellationToken`** — This is critical. If it blocks on a `BlockingCollection`, use `Take(token)`. If it blocks on I/O, use async overloads. Without this, cancellation stalls.

### Critical pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **`GetNextItem()` ignores the token** | Thread hangs forever — cancellation only works if blocking calls observe it |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and callbacks |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the top-level loop; let it propagate elsewhere |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
| **No fallback for uncooperative code** | If you truly can't pass the token through, move work to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()` |
